---
<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="45–55 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Jobs &amp; <em>workplaces</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Talk about a person’s work without mixing up the <strong>role</strong>, <strong>workplace</strong>, <strong>employer</strong>, and <strong>responsibilities</strong>. Compare <span class="ex">She’s a nurse</span>, <span class="ex">She works at Northside Clinic</span>, <span class="ex">She works for Northside Health</span>, and <span class="ex">She helps patients</span>.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">45–55 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">a / an + role</span>
    <span class="tag">as · at · in · for · from home</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min optional listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Separate four kinds of work information.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Choose a or an from the first sound.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Connect roles, places, employers, and tasks.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Present a fictional team and ask clear questions.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">Work can be a useful conversation topic, but it is not always the right first question. A person can answer briefly, choose a different topic, or use a safe status such as <span class="ex">I’m a student</span>, <span class="ex">I’m retired</span>, or <span class="ex">I’m not working right now</span>.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a clock phrase, a third-person present form, and at before an exact time.">
    <h3>Three quick checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Answer from memory, then check. Say each complete sentence aloud.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Answer choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="half past">half past</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="quarter to">quarter to</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="work">work</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="works">works</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at">at</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The digital clock shows 7:30: It is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="half past" data-hint="Thirty minutes after seven is half past seven." data-fix="7:30 is half past seven." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> seven. <span class="contraction">(telling the time)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The routine belongs to one person: Maya <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="works" data-hint="The third-person singular subject Maya takes works." data-fix="Maya works every weekday." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> every weekday. <span class="contraction">(present simple)</span></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The meeting card gives the exact time 9:00: The meeting starts <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at" data-hint="Use at before an exact clock time." data-fix="The meeting starts at nine." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> nine. <span class="contraction">(prepositions of time)</span></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet the roles</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Use this as an optional word bank. Say each role in a complete chunk: <span class="ex">She’s a doctor. He works as a cook.</span> The last card, <span class="ex">student</span>, describes study status and is not necessarily a paid job.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/doctor.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">doctor</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/teacher.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">teacher</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/cook.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">cook</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/police-officer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">police officer</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/firefighter.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">firefighter</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/farmer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">farmer</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/mechanic.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">mechanic</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/office-worker.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">office worker</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/scientist.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">scientist</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/artist.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">artist</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/pilot.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">pilot</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/construction-worker.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">construction worker</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/factory-worker.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">factory worker</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/programmer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">programmer</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/singer.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">singer</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/astronaut.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">astronaut</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/judge.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">judge</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/student.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">student</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Meet the workplaces</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>These are physical settings. Say the complete chunks: <span class="ex">at a hospital</span>, <span class="ex">in an office</span>, and <span class="ex">from home</span>. A <span class="ex">store</span> is the usual U.S. English word for a place that sells things.</p>
  <div class="figure-grid">
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/office.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">office</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/hospital.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">hospital</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/school.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">school</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/factory.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">factory</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/store.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">store</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/bank.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">bank</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/hotel.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">hotel</span></div>
    <div class="figure"><span class="fig"><img src="/assets/lessons/img/noto/house.svg" alt="" width="62" height="62" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></span><span class="nm">home</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice before the rule</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Four lines from a fictional welcome event</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“I’m Nia. I’m a nurse. I work at Northside Clinic. I work for Northside Health, and I help patients.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which words name Nia’s <strong>role</strong>? Which name the <strong>workplace</strong>?</li>
      <li>Which words name the <strong>employer</strong>? Which words describe a regular <strong>responsibility</strong>?</li>
      <li>Why does <span class="ex">nurse</span> have <span class="ex">a</span>? What would change before <span class="ex">engineer</span>?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You chose a or an from the first spoken sound, including the /y/ sound in university.">
    <h3>Discovery check: choose a or an from the sound</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Say the next word aloud. Use <b>an</b> before a vowel sound and <b>a</b> before a consonant sound.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Article choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="a">a</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="an">an</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The profile says Nia works in patient care: She’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="Nurse begins with the consonant sound /n/." data-fix="She’s a nurse." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> nurse.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The profile says Omar designs machines: He’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an" data-hint="Engineer begins with a vowel sound." data-fix="He’s an engineer." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> engineer.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The profile says Zoe prepares financial reports: She’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an" data-hint="Accountant begins with a vowel sound." data-fix="She’s an accountant." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> accountant.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The profile says Luis leads the office team: He’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="Manager begins with the consonant sound /m/." data-fix="He’s a manager." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> manager.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The profile says Ava installs electrical systems: She’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="an" data-hint="Electrician begins with a vowel sound." data-fix="She’s an electrician." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> electrician.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The study profile says Ben attends college: He’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="a" data-hint="University begins with the consonant sound /y/, as in you." data-fix="He’s a university student." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> university student.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: four kinds of work information</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>A job conversation becomes easier when you decide what information you want. The same person can give four different answers because each answer has a different focus.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>information</th><th>question</th><th>answer pattern</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>occupation or role</b></td><td><span class="ex">What do you do?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I’m a nurse. I work as a nurse.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>workplace</b></td><td><span class="ex">Where do you work?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I work at Northside Clinic.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>employer</b></td><td><span class="ex">Who do you work for?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I work for Northside Health.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>responsibilities</b></td><td><span class="ex">What do you do at work?</span></td><td><span class="ex">I help patients.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line"><span class="ex">What do you do?</span> commonly asks about occupation during an introduction. In a different situation, it can ask about an action. Context creates the meaning. Compare a welcome event with someone asking what to do during an emergency.</p>

  <h3>Role, workplace, employer, department, or arrangement?</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>pattern</th><th>use</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>work as + a/an + role</b></td><td>name the role</td><td><span class="ex">I work as an accountant.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>work at + named place</b></td><td>present an organization or location as the workplace point</td><td><span class="ex">I work at City Hospital.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>work in + setting, department, or field</b></td><td>look inside an environment or area of work</td><td><span class="ex">I work in an office. I work in sales.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>work for + employer or client</b></td><td>name the organization or person that employs the worker</td><td><span class="ex">I work for SunTech.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>work from home</b></td><td>name a remote-work arrangement</td><td><span class="ex">I work from home on Fridays.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>At and in can overlap</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">She works at a hospital</span> presents the hospital as her workplace. <span class="ex">She works in a hospital</span> presents the setting she works inside. Both can be natural. Use <span class="ex">in</span> clearly for a department or field: <span class="ex">in the billing department</span>, <span class="ex">in education</span>. Use <span class="ex">at</span> clearly when naming a workplace point: <span class="ex">at Northside Clinic</span>. Do not treat every <span class="ex">at/in</span> difference as a right-or-wrong choice.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>A singular role needs a marker</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use <span class="ex">a/an</span> with one singular countable role: <span class="ex">I’m a nurse. She’s an engineer.</span></li>
      <li>Do not use <span class="ex">a/an</span> before a plural role: <span class="ex">They’re nurses.</span></li>
      <li>Choose the article by the first <strong>sound</strong>, not the first letter: <span class="ex">an accountant</span> but <span class="ex">a university student</span> because <em>university</em> starts with /y/.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h3>Roles connect to tasks, but titles vary</h3>
  <p>The examples below are useful starting points, not universal definitions. The same title can involve different tasks in different workplaces. A <strong>cook</strong> prepares food; a <strong>chef</strong> may also plan menus or lead a kitchen, but workplaces use these titles differently.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>role</th><th>possible responsibility</th><th>possible setting</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>teacher</b></td><td>teaches classes</td><td>school or online</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>nurse</b></td><td>helps patients</td><td>clinic or hospital</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>cook</b></td><td>prepares food</td><td>restaurant or cafeteria</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>mechanic</b></td><td>repairs vehicles</td><td>repair shop</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>programmer</b></td><td>writes and tests software</td><td>office or home</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>server</b></td><td>takes orders and brings food</td><td>restaurant</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>receptionist</b></td><td>greets visitors and answers calls</td><td>office, clinic, or hotel</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. Each fictional staff profile now has the one responsibility stated in its work notes.">
    <h3>Responsibility check: what does each person do at work?</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use the stated task, not a stereotype about every person with that job.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Work responsibility choices">
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="helps patients">helps patients</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="teaches classes">teaches classes</button>
      <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="repairs cars">repairs cars</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="writes software">writes software</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Nia’s clinic profile says “patient care”: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="helps patients" data-hint="Patient care means that Nia helps patients." data-fix="Nia is a nurse, and she helps patients." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Mr. Lee’s schedule lists three English classes: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="teaches classes" data-hint="The schedule lists classes that Mr. Lee teaches." data-fix="Mr. Lee is a teacher, and he teaches classes." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Rosa’s garage card lists brake and engine repairs: She <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="repairs cars" data-hint="Brake and engine repairs are work on cars." data-fix="Rosa is a mechanic, and she repairs cars." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Dev’s project list names a new phone app: He <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="writes software" data-hint="A phone app is software that a programmer can write." data-fix="Dev is a programmer, and he writes software." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div>
    <p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: stress, linking, and third-person endings</h3>
    <p>The spellings below are simple sound guides, not new spellings to write. Put one clear beat in each longer role word.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>word</th><th>sound guide</th><th>spoken detail</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>engineer</td><td><span class="ex">en-juh-<strong>NEER</strong></span></td><td>link the article: <span class="contraction">an-engineer</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>accountant</td><td><span class="ex">uh-<strong>COUNT</strong>-uhnt</span></td><td>the first and last vowel sounds are light</td></tr>
        <tr><td>electrician</td><td><span class="ex">ih-lek-<strong>TRISH</strong>-uhn</span></td><td>the ending <span class="ex">-cian</span> sounds like <span class="ex">shuhn</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td>receptionist</td><td><span class="ex">ri-<strong>SEP</strong>-shuh-nist</span></td><td>the letters <span class="ex">-tion-</span> include a /sh/ sound</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Keep the linking words light in <span class="ex">work as a</span>, <span class="ex">work at a</span>, and <span class="ex">work for a</span>. Give the new information the clearest beat: <span class="ex">I work as a <strong>COOK</strong>. I work for <strong>SUN</strong>Tech.</span></p>
    <p>Keep the third-person ending audible. <span class="ex">works</span> ends with /s/. <span class="ex">teaches</span> adds a final syllable /ɪz/. Compare <span class="ex">I work</span> with <span class="ex">She works</span>, and <span class="ex">I teach</span> with <span class="ex">He teaches</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read role, workplace, employer, department, and remote-work listening check">
    <h2>Listen: what kind of work information did you hear?</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Your teacher reads each fictional line once with natural U.S. rhythm and once more slowly. Choose the information focus. Open the script only after checking.</p>
    <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You distinguished a job title, role, named workplace, department, employer, and remote arrangement.">
      <h3>Tutor-read listening check</h3>
      <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Work information meanings">
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="job title">job title</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="role">role</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="named workplace">named workplace</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="department">department</button>
        <button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="employer">employer</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="remote arrangement">remote arrangement</button>
      </div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>Clip 1 gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="job title" data-hint="The pattern be plus a nurse directly names the job title." data-fix="She’s a nurse: job title." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Clip 2 gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="role" data-hint="Work as introduces the person’s role." data-fix="She works as a nurse: role." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>Clip 3 gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="named workplace" data-hint="At plus Northside Clinic names the workplace point." data-fix="She works at Northside Clinic: named workplace." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>Clip 4 gives a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="department" data-hint="In the billing department names an area inside the organization." data-fix="She works in the billing department: department." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
      <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>Clip 5 gives an <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="employer" data-hint="Work for introduces the employer." data-fix="She works for Northside Health: employer." data-placeholder="listen">listen</button>.</span></div>
