  <h2>Use it safely and flexibly</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real job, employer, workplace, income, schedule, coworkers, or employment status. Use the fictional profiles below, invent details, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Question choice:</b> Your partner has a fictional profile. Ask one question about the role, one about the workplace, one about the employer, and one about responsibilities. Use <span class="ex">What do you do?</span>, <span class="ex">Where do you work?</span>, <span class="ex">Who do you work for?</span>, and <span class="ex">What do you do at work?</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Flexible answers:</b> Practice three honest status answers without adding private details: <span class="ex">I’m a student. I’m retired. I’m not working right now.</span> Then move the conversation to a comfortable topic.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>At or in:</b> Explain the difference in viewpoint between <span class="ex">works at a hospital</span> and <span class="ex">works in a hospital</span>. Give one clear department example with <span class="ex">in</span>.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: introduce a fictional community-center team for 45–60 seconds.</b> Include two people, two roles with <span class="ex">a/an</span>, one named workplace, one employer, one department or field, two responsibilities, one remote arrangement, and one clear question for a new team member.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Tutor extension:</b> Add one new fictional profile. The learner reformulates it twice, first as a short social introduction and then as a more detailed staff-directory entry.</div>

